// expect-ok
// Z3 corpus #35: axis-1 — Wall 4 Step 3 semantic equivalence over i64.
//
// WHAT THIS FIXTURE PROVES
//
// `value > -1` and `copy >= 0` define the same integer set on i64
// (since x > -1 ⇔ x >= 0 for integers). Step 2 emitted T215 here
// because `(Gt, -1) != (Ge, 0)` syntactically. Step 3's V14 amendment
// (via V33) admits Z3-backed implication: `∃x. (x > -1) ∧ ¬(x >= 0)`
// has no integer solution → Unsat → subsumption holds → accept.
//
// This is the "different op + different literal, semantically
// equivalent" case. It's the cleanest demonstration that Step 3 is
// NOT just "syntactic equality with a different shape" but actual
// semantic implication.
//
// THE LANGUAGE PRIMITIVES THIS NEEDED
//
// Same as fixture 34 — Step 2 attachment + Step 3 V33 dispatcher.
// The encoding details (operator translation in
// `check_refinement_subsumption`) carry the semantic equivalence
// through to Z3 unchanged.
//
// MUTATION CONTRACT
//
// Not applicable (expect-ok). Replacing the destination with
// `where copy > 0` (strict positive) would flip this into a T215
// case because (x > -1) does NOT imply (x > 0); x=0 is a
// counterexample.
module sigil;

record Almost { value: i64 } where value > -1

record NonNeg { copy: i64 } where copy >= 0

entry actor Main {
    on Tick() -> i64 {
        let a: Almost = Almost { value: 100 };
        let n: NonNeg = NonNeg { copy: a.value };
        return 1;
    }
}
